00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.000
So the first thing when
we upload a picture is we

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:19.800
need a place to
store our pictures.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:22.430
So that's where Google Cloud
Storage comes in, or cloud

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:24.100
storage for Firebase.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:27.330
As said, they're both
the same product.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:30.830
The product is actually a highly
available, durable, simple and

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:32.127
secure object storage.

00:05:32.127 --> 00:05:32.960
What does that mean?

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:34.370
What is an object storage?

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:37.370
So object storage is pretty
much every kind of blob

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:38.630
that you can think of.

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:40.850
A blob is basically bytes.

00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:42.140
So it can be images.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:43.560
It can be videos.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:44.730
It can be normal files.

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:50.390
It can pretty much be any
byte stream that you can find.

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:53.540
Now, cloud storage is
optimized for user latency,

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:54.560
for end user latency.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:56.660
There are a lot of
things that you can do.

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:59.420
You can define the caching
strategy, things like that.

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:04.100
You can make your object storage
public so that people outside

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:05.060
can access it.

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:06.560
You can actually
make it in a sense

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:09.200
that other people
pay for the access

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.820
to your cloud storage system.

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:18.090
It is integrated with over
100 end points, or cloud POPs

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:18.770
that we call.

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:21.530
So basically if somebody
comes in from, let's say,

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:25.190
Asia or Europe, they
go to the closest POP,

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:27.720
which is connected to their
internet service provider.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.330
And then they go directly
into our Google network.

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:32.930
And that means the latency
to retrieve objects, or even

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:35.370
store objects, are minimized.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:37.820
So it's a really good
tool for our app.

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:39.750
That's exactly what
we want and need.

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:42.410
All right, so we're going to
start with creating a bucket.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:46.340
So a bucket is a unique
route end point, so to say,

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:50.280
that you have to create to
be able to store some data.

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:52.310
When you create a bucket,
you will define things

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:55.760
like the storage
class and the location

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:57.330
where your bucket lives.

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:01.030
So in this case, as you can
see here, I have two examples.

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:04.010
Either you use our
CLI with gsutil.

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:05.000
You make a bucket.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:06.380
You define the storage class.

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:08.090
In this case, it's
multi-regional.

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:12.890
And then my storage
is cloudy-pictures.

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:15.250
You can do the same with
Java if you want to.

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:17.360
So if you want to create
buckets programmatically,

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:22.130
you can use our storage APIs,
and then basically directly

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:25.430
created from your Java program.

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:27.470
All right, the next step
is uploading a file.

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:29.330
Again, very easy if
you're familiar with

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:32.090
CP on any kind of
operating system.

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:35.960
You just prepend
it with gsutil-cm,

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:40.640
which allows you to actually
multicast your upload.

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:42.800
And then you upload
a picture here

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:44.990
to our cloud storage bucket.

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:49.910
In Java, pretty much simple
and straightforward as well.

00:07:49.910 --> 00:07:52.510
You select the file,
or you point to a file.

00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:55.010
You make a byte area out of it.

00:07:55.010 --> 00:07:57.710
You put it in a bucket,
and create it with a name,

00:07:57.710 --> 00:07:59.000
as you can see here.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:01.890
In this example, I actually
also gave it the content type

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:04.910
that you can do as well.

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:07.050
If you want to do
it from the web,

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:10.160
for the ones that have done
Firebase, this looks familiar.

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:13.850
So basically, you use
the Firebase web SDKs.

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:16.760
You get the storage reference.

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:19.960
You use a file reader.

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:23.750
From your input field you get
the file name and the content.

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:26.090
And you upload that to storage--

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:27.260
very straightforward.

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:30.670
That we use as well for our app.

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:35.147
And next, last but not least for
storage, is how to read a file.

00:08:35.147 --> 00:08:36.730
And, again, it's
very straightforward.

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:39.000
You can do a copy,
like download it.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.850
You can use Java,
the blob object,

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:43.780
and read it from your bucket.

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:46.500
Or on the web, you basically
get a download URL.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:49.140
And then you use
this download URL,

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:51.120
and put it in, for
instance, your image tag,

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:55.300
or your background
image CSS attribute.

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:57.840
All right, so that's great.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:01.720
We uploaded all our pictures,
or we uploaded one picture.

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:04.440
Now, how can I do processing?

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:08.640
If I upload a picture from
my phone, or from my camera,

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:10.050
these pictures are large.

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:11.280
They are like megabytes.

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:13.530
And I don't want to serve
megabytes of pictures

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:16.740
always to my web
page or to my users.

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:19.579
That would strain
the bandwidth a lot.

00:09:19.579 --> 00:09:21.620
So what I want to do is
I want to actually create

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:23.497
some thumbnails.

00:09:23.497 --> 00:09:25.830
Now, there is the possibility
that you create thumbnails

00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:27.420
already on your clients.

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:29.200
But I don't think
that is a good idea.

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:31.920
There are many reasons
for not doing this.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:34.270
Now, I can create
thumbnails on the back end,

00:09:34.270 --> 00:09:35.310
which I'm going to use.

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:38.550
And I could trigger
the thumbnail creation

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:39.660
from my clients.

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:41.700
I don't think that is
a good idea either.

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:43.440
What happens if you
upload a picture,

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:48.210
and then you get disconnected,
or the thumbnail creation fails

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:49.830
and you need to retry it?

00:09:49.830 --> 00:09:52.980
All of this logic would be
cluttering your front end

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:54.630
and your client code.

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:56.530
You want to do these
things on the back end.

00:09:56.530 --> 00:09:58.410
So I want to use a
different mechanism

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:02.910
to actually trigger the
processing of my image files.

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:05.760
And that's where object
notifications come in.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:08.400
So object notifications
on Google Cloud Storage

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:12.900
allows you to basically
register a hook which

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:16.690
gets called if anything
happens on your storage system.

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:18.930
So there are four
event types that you

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:21.586
can hook your systems up to.

00:10:21.586 --> 00:10:25.140
It's also finalized, which
is basically object create.

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:26.620
It's an object delete.

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:28.290
And it's archiving of an object.

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:30.090
And it's any kind
of metadata update

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:31.870
that you do on your objects.

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:34.450
So you can hook into
any of these events.

00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:36.240
And there are different
mechanisms to hook

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:37.670
into these events.

00:10:37.670 --> 00:10:40.950
There are three main
mechanisms that you can use.

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:43.560
On one hand, if you use
Firebase and Cloud Functions,

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:47.010
you can use the cloud storage
trigger for Cloud Functions.

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:50.310
So basically, if you
submit your function,

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:53.430
you can say these functions
get triggered if anything

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:56.670
happens on my bucket.

00:10:56.670 --> 00:10:59.770
This is actually integrated with
the Cloud Pub/Sub notification

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:00.270
system.

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:02.670
So Cloud Pup/Sub
notification also

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:08.190
allows you to set a notification
watch command on your bucket

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:10.740
and say, if anything
happens of these events

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:14.020
that I declare, please
send me a Pub/Sub message.

00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:15.990
And then you can
have a system that

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:17.910
deals with the Pub/Sub message.

00:11:17.910 --> 00:11:21.750
And kind of the legacy object
change notifications that

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:24.000
are still around are
what we introduced

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:28.230
very early on in the Google
Cloud storage product.

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:30.240
It's the object
change notification.

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:32.370
So if you register an
object change notification,

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:36.420
you'll basically get an HTTPS
call to an HTTPS end point.

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:39.880
And then you can deal with
the message there as well.

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:41.760
The nice thing about
this is all of this

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:43.830
what you're using here,
if you set this up,

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:47.230
is integrate in an array
that it does all the retry.

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:50.190
So if you don't acknowledge
a successful processing

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:53.790
of the message, the system
just retries it automatically,

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:56.382
up to 30 days.

00:11:56.382 --> 00:11:59.790
All right, so how do I
setup Cloud Pub/Sub trigger?

00:11:59.790 --> 00:12:00.780
Here's an example.

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:06.480
I'm using the gcloud
Pub/Sub gcloud SDK, or CLI.

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:09.600
So I do gcloud pubsub
topics create notifs.

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:11.610
I declare a notification.

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:14.010
I create here, in this
case, a notification

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:18.630
against my cloudy
