fields or things like that,

00:16:57.090 --> 00:17:00.630
you can do that throughout the
lifecycle of your application.

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:03.450
So you don't have to be very
restrictive on the schema

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:04.750
that you are going to use.

00:17:04.750 --> 00:17:07.290
You can just evolve
the schema while you

00:17:07.290 --> 00:17:09.460
develop your application.

00:17:09.460 --> 00:17:12.109
Now, for Firestore, again,
they are client libraries

00:17:12.109 --> 00:17:15.630
which you can use from the web,
as well as from your back end

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:17.619
systems.

00:17:17.619 --> 00:17:20.380
So Cloud Firestore,
just to summarize again,

00:17:20.380 --> 00:17:21.650
it's very flexible.

00:17:21.650 --> 00:17:25.060
You have an expressive
query language.

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:26.349
You get real time updates.

00:17:26.349 --> 00:17:29.680
If you do Firebased, you can
basically use watch commands

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:32.840
or basically get events for
any data that has changed.

00:17:32.840 --> 00:17:35.440
You have offline support if
you do a web application.

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:37.060
And it's designed
to scale again.

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:41.960
So this is a system that
can scale to massive sizes.

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:44.860
So how do you write
data to Firestore?

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:46.720
Here's an example for Java.

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:50.080
So pretty much everything
in Firestore is async.

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:52.780
So as you can see here,
you get a document graph.

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:57.549
You give it a name to your
document, basically an ID.

00:17:57.549 --> 00:17:58.840
You have a collection document.

00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:00.610
And then you put
your fields in there.

00:18:00.610 --> 00:18:03.230
To write this, you
use an API future.

00:18:03.230 --> 00:18:06.550
And then you can also update
fields if you want to.

00:18:06.550 --> 00:18:09.640
Now, if you read data, it's
pretty much similar to that.

00:18:09.640 --> 00:18:12.360
There are not a lot
of changes here.

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:15.000
On the document ref that you
create, you just do a get.

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:17.850
And that blocks till
you have the data.

