00:25:19.550 --> 00:25:22.070
are at least 100 miles
apart from each other,

00:25:22.070 --> 00:25:25.590
and gives you the highest
availability for your needs.

00:25:25.590 --> 00:25:27.230
Now, if you do data
processing, and you

00:25:27.230 --> 00:25:29.180
want to actually
process your data

00:25:29.180 --> 00:25:31.760
close to where it
is living, then we

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:33.750
recommend to use
a regional bucket.

00:25:33.750 --> 00:25:36.440
The regional bucket
is one region.

00:25:36.440 --> 00:25:39.830
It's a little bit cheaper, and
it gives you a little bit less

00:25:39.830 --> 00:25:42.320
of an availability SLA.

00:25:42.320 --> 00:25:44.600
Now, when it comes
to archiving data,

00:25:44.600 --> 00:25:47.560
we have a nearline and a
coldline storage class.

00:25:47.560 --> 00:25:51.890
Nearline is pretty much if your
images or any kind of objects

00:25:51.890 --> 00:25:55.790
are not used within 30 days,
you can move them to nearline.

00:25:55.790 --> 00:25:57.882
And it's cheaper
actually to use nearline

00:25:57.882 --> 00:26:00.090
than have them stored on a
regional or multi-regional

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:01.560
bucket.

00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:05.400
Now, if you use your data
less than once a year,

00:26:05.400 --> 00:26:08.090
then it's recommended to
move these to coldline.

00:26:08.090 --> 00:26:12.260
You still have the
same persistency

00:26:12.260 --> 00:26:14.630
of your data objects.

00:26:14.630 --> 00:26:17.300
And you still have the same
APIs, which is amazing.

00:26:17.300 --> 00:26:20.540
But the prices get up.

00:26:20.540 --> 00:26:22.400
Now, one thing which
is really important

00:26:22.400 --> 00:26:24.530
to do all these
things automated,

00:26:24.530 --> 00:26:26.900
or in your application,
is something called

00:26:26.900 --> 00:26:28.287
object lifecycle.

00:26:28.287 --> 00:26:29.870
With object lifecycle,
what you can do

00:26:29.870 --> 00:26:34.460
is you can define rules for when
the object gets automatically

00:26:34.460 --> 00:26:37.140
transitioned between
the storage classes.

00:26:37.140 --> 00:26:38.990
So on my bucket I
could define a rule.

00:26:38.990 --> 00:26:40.910
If an object is
older than 30 days,

00:26:40.910 --> 00:26:42.260
please move it to nearline.

00:26:42.260 --> 00:26:46.630
If it is older than like 365
days, or older than 90 days,

00:26:46.630 --> 00:26:50.100
please move it to coldline.

00:26:50.100 --> 00:26:52.340
But I also can do
that per object.

00:26:52.340 --> 00:26:55.730
I don't have to create a bucket
with a specific storage class.

00:26:55.730 --> 00:26:56.820
You can do that.

00:26:56.820 --> 00:26:57.890
But you don't have to.

00:26:57.890 --> 00:27:01.397
You can actually change the
storage class per object.

00:27:01.397 --> 00:27:02.480
So what does it look like?

00:27:02.480 --> 00:27:05.570
You can use it either with
gsutil and do it there.

00:27:05.570 --> 00:27:10.850
Or you can use a Java, as you
see there, to move your object.

00:27:10.850 --> 00:27:13.580
So I'm going to show that
really quickly how we're

00:27:13.580 --> 00:27:15.600
going to do this here.

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:19.560
So I have a second
application here,

00:27:19.560 --> 00:27:22.700
which extracts
some more metadata.

00:27:22.700 --> 00:27:26.610
So as you can see here, the
same setup code pretty much.

00:27:26.610 --> 00:27:29.090
But what I do is I'm
reading the object,

00:27:29.090 --> 00:27:34.670
and then I'm using the SDK
to get EXIF data out of it.

00:27:34.670 --> 00:27:36.800
So now when this is
done, what I want to do

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:38.960
is actually I want to
move this object away.

00:27:41.490 --> 00:27:45.660
So let's see how we can do this
so we can actually do it here,

00:27:45.660 --> 00:27:48.170
and then move the object.

00:27:55.164 --> 00:27:56.705
Let's see how we're
going to do this.

00:28:02.790 --> 00:28:04.360
All right, there it is.

00:28:04.360 --> 00:28:06.700
So basically I take my object.

00:28:06.700 --> 00:28:08.880
I just define a
new storage class,

00:28:08.880 --> 00:28:12.160
build this, and then say, OK,
when my metadata is processed,

00:28:12.160 --> 00:28:14.690
I'm just going to
move it to nearline.

00:28:14.690 --> 00:28:17.060
So I'm going to start
this here as well.

00:28:17.060 --> 00:28:20.840
And that's going to listen
on my notification as well.

00:28:20.840 --> 00:28:23.590
So now, this is a topic
where notifications

00:28:23.590 --> 00:28:24.940
have been sent before.

00:28:24.940 --> 00:28:28.570
So as you can see, it starts
actually processing as well.

00:28:28.570 --> 00:28:30.400
And it might throw
some errors because I

00:28:30.400 --> 00:28:33.470
have some deleted some object.

00:28:33.470 --> 00:28:37.970
But it basically will process,
in the background, our object.

00:28:37.970 --> 00:28:40.790
So let's have a look if
that is actually working.

00:28:40.790 --> 00:28:44.320
So what we can have here
is we have our objects.

00:28:44.320 --> 00:28:49.580
And in time, what you can see
here is metadata is showing up.

00:28:49.580 --> 00:28:51.580
So this gives me
all the EXIF data,

00:28:51.580 --> 00:28:53.590
which I now can use
to search for instants

00:28:53.590 --> 00:28:57.370
or build a world map where
I can put all my pictures.

00:28:57.370 --> 00:29:01.140
And if I go to storage,
which I'm going to do here--

00:29:01.140 --> 00:29:03.460
I'm going to go to
my storage system.

00:29:03.460 --> 00:29:06.130
I'm going to go to
cloudy pictures.

00:29:06.130 --> 00:29:08.730
I'm going to go to my raws.

00:29:08.730 --> 00:29:09.700
Going to see here.

00:29:09.700 --> 00:29:12.460
And as you can see,
my pictures actually

00:29:12.460 --> 00:29:15.600
now have the storage
system nearline.

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:17.100
So even though I
created this bucket

00:29:17.100 --> 00:29:21.100
as a multi-regional bucket,
I'm already moving my objects

00:29:21.100 --> 00:29:24.067
into a different storage class,
which is really powerful.

00:29:24.067 --> 00:29:25.150
All right, switching back.

00:29:28.750 --> 00:29:30.020
All right, so we have that.

00:29:30.020 --> 00:29:32.420
We have reduced our costs
by moving our objects

00:29:32.420 --> 00:29:34.020
and saving some costs there.

00:29:34.020 --> 00:29:37.050
The next thing that I want to
do is enable a business model.

00:29:37.050 --> 00:29:40.040
I want to generate
some money with this.

00:29:40.040 --> 00:29:42.530
So the app has gone viral.

00:29:42.530 --> 00:29:43.970
I have stored a lot of pictures.

00:29:43.970 --> 00:29:45.980
And now people
have asked and want

00:29:45.980 --> 00:29:50.570
to actually print and
frame, so buy a frame

00:29:50.570 --> 00:29:51.980
and ship the pictures.

00:29:51.980 --> 00:29:53.480
So I want to enable this.

00:29:53.480 --> 00:29:55.704
Now, if I want to do this,
I'm dealing with money.

00:29:55.704 --> 00:29:57.620
And if I'm dealing with
money, I need actually

00:29:57.620 --> 00:29:59.771
a transactional system.

00:29:59.771 --> 00:30:01.520
So I don't want to do
this with Firestore,

00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:03.740
because I think my
transactional guarantees that I

00:30:03.740 --> 00:30:06.230
have with Firestore are
not sufficient for what

00:30:06.230 --> 00:30:07.560
I want to do.

00:30:07.560 --> 00:30:10.490
So that's where another
storage system comes in.

00:30:10.490 --> 00:30:14.420
And we have a managed
storage system for Cloud SQL.

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:17.470
So Cloud SQL is a
managed RDBMS system.

00:30:17.470 --> 00:30:21.170
You can have MySQL or
Postgres as a flavor.

00:30:21.170 --> 00:30:24.870
And it gives you things
like automatic updates.

00:30:24.870 --> 00:30:25.790
You can do backups.

00:30:25.790 --> 00:30:27.020
You can do fail overs.

00:30:27.020 --> 00:30:29.630
You can do fail overs
into other people clouds.

00:30:29.630 --> 00:30:32.930
You can do fail overs into
on premise systems, things

00:30:32.930 --> 00:30:33.860
like that.

00:30:33.860 --> 00:30:35.330
It is vertically scalable.

00:30:35.330 --> 00:30:38.990
So you can change your node
size throughout the lifecycle

00:30:38.990 --> 00:30:40.280
of your application.

00:30:40.280 --> 00:30:44.660
And you have things
like a 99.95 SLA.

00:30:44.660 --> 00:30:46.700
So how do we create
a MySQL instance?

00:30:46.700 --> 00:30:50.430
Again, very straightforward--
gcloud SQL instance create.

00:30:50.430 --> 00:30:51.380
You give it a name.

00:30:51.380 --> 00:30:52.880
You say the tier,
which is basically

00:30:52.880 --> 00:30:55.463
the size that you want to use,
and the region where this cloud

00:30:55.463 --> 00:30:58.340
SQL instance is created.

00:30:58.340 --> 00:31:03.810
Next thing is I want to talk
to my cloud SQL instance.

00:31:03.810 --> 00:31:06.860
So what we provide
is actually a proxy

00:31:06.860 --> 00:31:09.140
which automatically
authenticates you

00:31:09.140 --> 00:31:12.287
if you provided a service file.

00:31:12.287 --> 00:31:14.120
So here what you can
see is you're basically

00:31:14.120 --> 00:31:16.400
down your cloud SQL proxy.

00:31:16.400 --> 00:31:17.810
And then you make it executable.

00:31:17.810 --> 00:31:19.670
And then you can run
it either locally,

00:31:19.670 --> 00:31:22.310
or you can run it
on your GC instance.

00:31:22.310 --> 00:31:24.770
Or if you are familiar
with Kubernetes,

00:31:24.770 --> 00:31:26.600
which is a container
management system,

00:31:26.600 --> 00:31:30.260
you can run it directly
in your parts as a sidecar

00:31:30.260 --> 00:31:32.990
to connect to your
cloud instance.

00:31:32.990 --> 00:31:34.880
That's pretty much
straight forward.

00:31:34.880 --> 00:31:40.740
Who here has done something
with MySQL or any SQL database?

00:31:40.740 --> 00:31:41.300
All right.

00:31:41.300 --> 00:31:42.270
Amazing.

00:31:42.270 --> 00:31:45.470
So this looks very familiar.

00:31:45.470 --> 00:31:46.340
Relational schema.

00:31:46.340 --> 00:31:50.090
I'm creating a database
and some tables.

00:31:50.090 --> 00:31:52.815
In this case, I'm
creating a user table,

00:31:52.815 --> 00:31:54.000
the Cloudaficionado.

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:56.000
I'm creating a balance
table, which is basically

00:31:56.000 --> 00:31:57.050
my account balance.

00:31:57.050 --> 00:31:59.120
And I'm creating a
transaction table,

00:31:59.120 --> 00:32:02.030
which shows me the history
of the transactions that

00:32:02.030 --> 00:32:03.420
have been done.

00:32:03.420 --> 00:32:08.690
And then I can do things
like a transaction that

00:32:08.690 --> 00:32:10.580
goes across multiple tables.

00:32:10.580 --> 00:32:12.860
So here you can see I have
a transaction that actually

00:32:12.860 --> 00:32:15.140
takes some money from
the balance table,

00:32:15.140 --> 00:32:17.030
puts it somewhere else
in the balance table,

00:32:17.030 --> 00:32:18.870
and also writes in
my transaction table

00:32:18.870 --> 00:32:21.170
that this has happened.

00:32:21.170 --> 00:32:23.630
So it's pretty much
straight forward.

00:32:23.630 --> 00:32:28.370
Now, the next step
is my app goes viral.

00:32:28.370 --> 00:32:34.260
So now what we want to do is
we need a scalability check.

00:32:34.260 --> 00:32:38.780
So when I build my
cloudy pictures 3.0,

00:32:38.780 --> 00:32:42.480
I want it to be scalable for
a large amount of people.

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:43.670
So let's check.

00:32:43.670 --> 00:32:46.790
Cloud storage is scalable.

00:32:46.790 --> 00:32:52.130
Cloud Firestore, or Firestore
on a Firebase is also scalable.

00:32:52.130 --> 00:32:53.900
It is a multitenant service.

00:32:53.900 --> 00:32:56.600
You don't have to
pre-provision any resources.

00:32:56.600 --> 00:32:58.960
You basically just use
it with your application,

00:32:58.960 --> 00:33:01.310
and the more you use, it
will scale with your needs.

00:33:01.310 --> 00:33:02.600
Amazing.

00:33:02.600 --> 00:33:05.330
Now, Cloud SQL, unfortunately,
is one solution that

00:33:05.330 --> 00:33:07.430
doesn't scale with your needs.

00:33:07.430 --> 00:33:09.950
You can scale it up
to a certain point.

00:33:09.950 --> 00:33:12.530
Basically the biggest VM
that your cloud provider,

00:33:12.530 --> 00:33:15.560
or, in our case, Google
Cloud Platform, provides you.

00:33:15.560 --> 00:33:18.980
But if you run out of the
size of the biggest VM,

00:33:18.980 --> 00:33:20.177
you're kind of stuck.

00:33:20.177 --> 00:33:22.010
There are many, many
things that you can do.

00:33:22.010 --> 00:33:26.570
You can, of course, do sharding,
and build like a distributed

00:33:26.570 --> 00:33:27.740
RDBMS.

00:33:27.740 --> 00:33:32.120
But it's very challenging and
costs a lot of time investment.

00:33:32.120 --> 00:33:34.580
And it's very risky.

00:33:34.580 --> 00:33:38.130
So now is where I want
to plug in Cloud Spanner.

00:33:38.130 --> 00:33:41.630
Cloud Spanner is our
horizontally scalable

00:33:41.630 --> 00:33:44.730
and distributed relational
database system.

00:33:44.730 --> 00:33:47.000
So you get strong
consistency with it,

00:33:47.000 --> 00:33:48.440
and it scales with your needs.

00:33:48.440 --> 00:33:50.720
You can scale to
thousands of nodes,

00:33:50.720 --> 00:33:54.502
and it scales horizontally
your schema, permitting.

00:33:54.502 --> 00:33:56.960
If you want to learn a little
bit more about Cloud Spanner,

00:33:56.960 --> 00:34:01.350
there's a shameless
plug at the bottom.

00:34:01.350 --> 00:34:04.430
So if you search for
TIL about Cloud Spanner

00:34:04.430 --> 00:34:06.170
you will find a
video series where

00:34:06.170 --> 00:34:10.429
I go through some of the nitty
gritties and amazing things

00:34:10.429 --> 00:34:13.489
that we do with Cloud Spanner.

00:34:13.489 --> 00:34:15.320
So let's recap.

00:34:15.320 --> 00:34:18.159
What is the portfolio under the
Google Cloud Storage or Google

00:34:18.159 --> 00:34:20.780
Cloud Platform that we have
in terms of data and storage

00:34:20.780 --> 00:34:21.889
products?

00:34:21.889 --> 00:34:23.960
So we have covered Cloud SQL.

00:34:23.960 --> 00:34:26.030
We have mentioned Cloud Spanner.

00:34:26.030 --> 00:34:29.300
We have mentioned Cloud
Data Store or Firestore.

00:34:29.300 --> 00:34:32.159
There are a couple of
others here on the slide.

00:34:32.159 --> 00:34:33.980
So if you use App
Engine, you can

00:34:33.980 --> 00:34:36.230
use, for instance,
App Engine Memcache.

00:34:36.230 --> 00:34:39.730
If you have a lot of data
which you want to search really

00:34:39.730 --> 00:34:41.540
fast, or process
really fast, you

00:34:41.540 --> 00:34:44.010
want to have a very
high read throughput,

00:34:44.010 --> 00:34:47.810
read and write throughput
for non-relational data,

00:34:47.810 --> 00:34:51.071
then we recommend to
use Cloud Bigtable.

00:34:51.071 --> 00:34:53.139
Cloud Bigtable has
an H base interface

00:34:53.139 --> 00:34:55.400
so you can connect it
to your like Spark,

00:34:55.400 --> 00:34:57.110
or things like that.

00:34:57.110 --> 00:35:00.320
Then we also have a data
warehouse system, a managed

00:35:00.320 --> 00:35:02.620
data warehouse system,
which is BigQuery,

00:35:02.620 --> 00:35:05.030
where you can go and
load relational data in,

00:35:05.030 --> 00:35:10.740
and crunch terabytes
of data with BigQuery.

00:35:10.740 --> 00:35:13.170
All right, so with
this, I thank you

00:35:13.170 --> 00:35:15.550
very much for your attention.

00:35:15.550 --> 00:35:17.670
If you have any
questions, please

00:35:17.670 --> 00:35:20.760
feel free to walk up
to the microphones.

00:35:20.760 --> 00:35:23.310
I'm happy to answer
some of your questions.

00:35:23.310 --> 00:35:27.090
And please also, if you want
to, follow me on Twitter

00:35:27.090 --> 00:35:28.530
under @hostirosti.

00:35:28.530 --> 00:35:30.890
And I'm happy to answer
any kinds of questions

00:35:30.890 --> 00:35:31.890
through Twitter as well.

00:35:31.890 --> 00:35:33.639
So thank you very much
for your attention.

00:35:33.639 --> 00:35:35.280
I hope you enjoyed the talk.

